# Status board: how each handler's runs are recorded.
#
# Every run is recorded, and by default its record is on disk before the run answers: the handler
# is strict. A handler that does nothing outside its run (it reads and answers) may say otherwise
# on its line:
#   lazy        recorded all the same, on disk within a second of the answer, so it answers
#               without waiting on the disk;
#   unrecorded  not in the log at all: its runs are counted, and only a failed one is kept. A
#               person approves it at deploy, "keep runs of GET /health out of its log".
# A handler that writes, calls out or waits is strict whatever it says: either word on one is an
# error at the line that does it.
#
# Try it: deploy it (holon deploy status-board.holon, approving what it asks), then
#   curl http://127.0.0.1:8170/health                                       "ok", counted
#   curl -X POST -d '{"id":"a","text":"hi"}' http://127.0.0.1:8170/notes    "saved", strict
#   curl http://127.0.0.1:8170/notes                                        the notes, lazy
# holon history status-board lists the two notes runs, and the health checks as one counted line.
# Put lazy on POST /notes and holon check says why it can't be.
flow status-board
port 8170
collection notes

# Answers the load balancer, every few seconds: not recorded, counted.
route GET /health (req) unrecorded {
  respond "ok"
}

# Lists the notes. Recorded, on disk within a second.
route GET /notes (req) lazy {
  respond notes.list(limit: 50)
}

# Adds a note. Strict, the default: its record is what the change is undone and explained by.
route POST /notes (req) {
  notes[req.json.id] = req.json.text
  respond "saved"
}

# The notes, for whoever asks by hand. Not recorded.
action report (input) unrecorded {
  respond notes.list(limit: 50)
}
